@doc """
A destination for log records. `handle_log` is the one required
method and runs in the process that emitted the record. A stdout
write is one syscall and belongs on that path. A handler that talks
to a network owns its own process, posts to it with `cast`, and
manages its own drop mode.

`Log.Config.handlers` holds closures, not protocol values, since a
list cannot mix handlers of two types. `to_fn` wraps a handler as
that closure.

```koja
Log.configure(Log.Config{handlers: [Log.Stdout.new(Log.Text{}).to_fn()]})
```

The method name carries the `_log` suffix so a process type that
implements `Log.Handler` keeps `handle` for its messages.
"""
@experimental
protocol Log.Handler
  @doc """
  Takes one record. Fails with the cause when the record did not
  reach the sink.
  """
  fn handle_log(self, record: Log.Record) ! Log.Error

  @doc """
  This handler as the closure `Log.Config.handlers` holds.
  """
  fn to_fn(self) -> fn (Log.Record) -> Result<(), Log.Error>
    handler = self

    fn (record: Log.Record) -> Result<(), Log.Error>
      handler.handle_log(record)
    end
  end
end

@doc """
Turns a record into one value of `T` for a handler to write. A text
handler takes a `Log.Formatter<String>` and a byte sink takes a
`Log.Formatter<Binary>`, so one socket handler carries any wire
format. The formatter allocates one `T` per record and the handler
owns framing such as the trailing newline.

The method name carries the `_log` suffix so a conforming struct
keeps the derived `Debug.format`.
"""
@experimental
protocol Log.Formatter<T>
  @doc """
  Renders one record.
  """
  fn format_log(self, record: Log.Record) -> T
end
